    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "The short answer is **no**. The fact that the interview panel was made up entirely of women is not, on its own, evidence of bias or a sign of an unfair process. Making an issue of the panel’s gender composition without concrete evidence would carry significant professional and reputational risk.\n\nHere is a breakdown of how to evaluate the situation objectively and what steps to take next:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Panel Demographics Are Not Evidence of Bias\n* **Organizational Structure:** Just as all-male interview panels have historically occurred due to executive composition, an all-female panel often simply reflects who currently holds the relevant stakeholder seats (e.g., Chief Risk Officer, Head of Compliance, Head of HR, Internal Audit).\n* **Legal and Evidentiary Reality:** In employment law and formal HR reviews, the demographic makeup of a panel does not constitute evidence of discrimination. Demonstrating unlawful bias requires showing inconsistent scoring, procedural irregularities, or discriminatory statements/behavior during the evaluation.\n\n### 2. The Nature of \"Head of Risk Modelling\" Roles\nAt the department-head level, hiring decisions rarely hinge purely on technical depth:\n* **Regulatory Focus:** In banking, model risk governance is under intense regulatory scrutiny (e.g., SR 11-7, TRIM, Basel IV, CCAR). If the bank recently faced regulatory scrutiny, audit findings, or remediation requirements, an applicant with deep **regulatory affairs** expertise and proven navigation with regulators may have matched their immediate strategic needs better than someone with stronger quantitative/theoretical depth.\n* **Executive & Soft Skills:** Department head roles heavily weight team leadership, executive communication, and cross-department negotiation alongside technical expertise.\n\n### 3. The Two-Week Delay on Feedback\n* A two-week wait for written feedback after senior-level hiring is standard in corporate banking. \n* Formal written feedback for executive candidates often requires sign-off from HR business partners and legal, leading to administrative delays. A two-week lapse is not an indicator of bad faith or avoidance.\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Send a Polite Follow-Up:**\n   Send a brief, constructive follow-up to the HR contact or recruiter:\n   > *\"I wanted to follow up on my request for feedback regarding the Head of Risk Modelling role. As I continue to target senior leadership opportunities, any insights on how I can better align my profile with organizational priorities would be greatly appreciated.\"*\n\n2. **Frame Feedback Around Strategic Gaps:**\n   If you receive feedback or secure a brief call, ask targeted questions:\n   * *Were there specific regulatory, managerial, or governance experiences they felt were missing from your profile?*\n   * *What strategic priorities tipped the balance toward the selected candidate?*\n\n3. **Protect Your Professional Standing:**\n   Risk modelling at senior levels is a relatively small community. Focusing on constructive takeaways and maintaining positive relations with the panel will serve your career far better than pursuing an unsubstantiated grievance.\n",
